A **price level** groups all resting orders at one price on one side. It stores at least the aggregate quantity and, for an exchange or a market-by-order book, a FIFO queue of the individual orders.

```cpp
using Price = std::int64_t;   // price in integer ticks, never double
using Qty   = std::int64_t;

struct Level {
    Price price;
    Qty   total_qty;          // sum of the queue: what market-by-price feeds publish
    // FIFO of orders at this price (for price-time priority)
};
```

| Feed | Content | Typical use |
|---|---|---|
| **L1** | Best bid/ask price and size only (BBO) | Simple quoting, displays |
| **L2 / market-by-price (MBP)** | Aggregate qty per price level, top N levels | Most strategies |
| **L3 / market-by-order (MBO)** | Every individual order with its id, add/modify/cancel/execute events | Queue-position modelling, exchange-side matching |

A trading firm usually rebuilds the book from the exchange feed (add / modify / delete / trade messages); the exchange runs the **matching engine** that owns the real book.

**Prices as integers:** store prices in ticks (e.g. 100.50 with tick 0.05 becomes 2010). Doubles are inexact (`0.1 + 0.2 != 0.3`), dangerous as map keys and for equality, and integer compares are faster.

| Message | Effect on the book |
|---|---|
| **Limit order** | Buy/sell up to qty at price P or better. The part that can trade trades now; the rest rests at P |
| **Market order** | Trade immediately at the best available prices, walking down the levels; no price limit; leftover usually cancelled |
| **IOC** (immediate-or-cancel) | Trade what you can now, cancel the rest; never rests |
| **FOK** (fill-or-kill) | Fill the whole qty immediately or nothing |
| **Post-only** | Rejected (or repriced) if it would trade immediately; only adds liquidity |
| **Cancel** | Remove a resting order by id |
| **Modify / amend** | Change qty or price of a resting order |

Modify rules matter for priority (typical exchange behaviour):

- **Reduce quantity** at the same price: keeps the order's place in the queue (implemented in place, O(1)).
- **Increase quantity** or **change price**: loses time priority; implemented as cancel + new order at the back of the queue.

The book interface **from class**:

```cpp
class OrderBook {
public:
    void addOrder(const Order& o);     // match, then rest the remainder
    void removeOrder(std::uint64_t id);// cancel
    /* ... */ getBids();               // levels for display / strategy
    /* ... */ getAsks();
    BBO getBBO();                      // top_most_bid, top_most_ask
};
```

**Matching rule:** an incoming buy at limit P trades against asks with price <= P, best (lowest) ask first, FIFO within a level. An incoming sell at limit P trades against bids with price >= P, highest first. The **trade price is the resting order's price** (the passive side sets the price).

**In class** (RELIANCE, all quantities in shares):

1. Kartik: BUY 10 @ 100. No asks, so it rests. Bids: `100: [K1 10]`.
2. Manik: SELL 5 @ 101. Best bid 100 < 101, no cross, rests. Asks: `101: [M 5]`.
3. Kartik: BUY 5 @ 100.5. Best ask 101 > 100.5, rests at a new, better level. Bids: `100.5: [K2 5]`, `100: [K1 10]`. BBO: 100.5 / 101.
4. Paras: SELL 10 @ 100. It crosses (100 <= best bid 100.5):
   - trades 5 @ **100.5** with K2 (level 100.5 now empty and removed),
   - next best bid 100 >= 100, trades 5 @ **100** with K1 (K1 has 5 left),
   - Paras is fully filled.

Final book: bids `100: [K1 5]`, asks `101: [M 5]`, spread 1.

```cpp
// core of matching an incoming buy (asks: map ascending, begin() = best)
while (in.qty > 0 && !asks.empty() && asks.begin()->first <= in.price) {
    auto& lvl = asks.begin()->second;          // FIFO of resting sells
    Order& rest = lvl.front();
    Qty q = std::min(in.qty, rest.qty);
    on_trade(in.id, rest.id, asks.begin()->first, q);   // trade at resting price
    in.qty -= q; rest.qty -= q;
    if (rest.qty == 0) { index.erase(rest.id); lvl.pop_front(); }
    if (lvl.empty()) asks.erase(asks.begin());
}
if (in.qty > 0) rest_in_book(in);              // remainder becomes the new best bid
```

A correct baseline you should be able to write in an exam:

```cpp
#include <cstdint>
#include <functional>
#include <list>
#include <map>
#include <unordered_map>

using Price = std::int64_t; using Qty = std::int64_t; using Id = std::uint64_t;
enum class Side { Buy, Sell };
struct Order { Id id; Side side; Price price; Qty qty; };

class OrderBook {
    using Fifo = std::list<Order>;
    std::map<Price, Fifo, std::greater<Price>> bids;   // best = begin()
    std::map<Price, Fifo>                      asks;   // best = begin()
    struct Loc { Side side; Price price; Fifo::iterator it; };
    std::unordered_map<Id, Loc> index;                 // id -> where it rests

    template <class Book, class Crosses>
    void match(Order& in, Book& book, Crosses crosses) {
        while (in.qty > 0 && !book.empty() && crosses(book.begin()->first)) {
            auto lvl = book.begin();
            Order& r = lvl->second.front();
            Qty q = std::min(in.qty, r.qty);
            in.qty -= q; r.qty -= q;                   // trade q @ lvl->first
            if (r.qty == 0) { index.erase(r.id); lvl->second.pop_front(); }
            if (lvl->second.empty()) book.erase(lvl);
        }
    }
public:
    void addOrder(Order o) {
        if (o.side == Side::Buy) match(o, asks, [&](Price p) { return p <= o.price; });
        else                     match(o, bids, [&](Price p) { return p >= o.price; });
        if (o.qty == 0) return;
        if (o.side == Side::Buy) { auto& f = bids[o.price]; f.push_back(o); index[o.id] = {o.side, o.price, std::prev(f.end())}; }
        else                     { auto& f = asks[o.price]; f.push_back(o); index[o.id] = {o.side, o.price, std::prev(f.end())}; }
    }
    void removeOrder(Id id) {
        auto it = index.find(id); if (it == index.end()) return;
        auto [side, px, pos] = it->second;
        if (side == Side::Buy) { auto l = bids.find(px); l->second.erase(pos); if (l->second.empty()) bids.erase(l); }
        else                   { auto l = asks.find(px); l->second.erase(pos); if (l->second.empty()) asks.erase(l); }
        index.erase(it);
    }
};
```

`std::list` iterators stay valid when other elements are inserted or erased, which is why storing them in the index is safe. This is correct but allocation-heavy; sessions 13-14 replace each piece with flat, pooled structures.

